WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.280 --> 00:00:03.960
Hello everyone. Welcome to the Langfocus channel.
My name is Paul.

00:00:04.060 --> 00:00:07.720
Today, I want to talk about: 
"Knowing your learning style".

00:00:07.820 --> 00:00:11.860
Most people rely on other people 
or on their materials to teach them.

00:00:11.980 --> 00:00:16.140
But I want you to be empowered to be able 
to direct your own language learning.

00:00:16.280 --> 00:00:21.500
That's why today we are going to look at your learning 
style, and how to use it to learn more efficiently.

00:00:21.640 --> 00:00:23.120
Different people learn differently

00:00:23.120 --> 00:00:28.700
We, all have a unique learning style that is based 
on the type of sensory information that we prefer.

00:00:28.820 --> 00:00:33.440
These kinds of sensory information are called 
learning modalities and there are four of them.

00:00:33.440 --> 00:00:35.700
Some people say 3, I'm going to say 4.

00:00:35.840 --> 00:00:40.780
They are: 
visual , auditory , reading and writing and kinesthetic.

00:00:41.020 --> 00:00:45.520
You can remember those learning modalities 
based on the acronym VARK.

00:00:45.940 --> 00:00:48.560
Students generally have a dominant modality.

00:00:48.640 --> 00:00:51.280
But they do use a combination 
of all the different modalities.

00:00:51.280 --> 00:00:54.260
Some people will say for example :
"I'm a visual learner".

00:00:54.280 --> 00:00:57.340
But really what they mean is that 
"I am primarily a visual learner"

00:00:57.400 --> 00:00:59.660
with some of the other modalities as well.

00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:06.880
There are some people who don't really have 
one strongly dominant modality and they call 
those people multimodal learners.

00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:09.080
They have a multi-modal learning style.

00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:13.980
I happen to be a multi-modal learner. 
I don't really have one really dominant modality.

00:01:14.020 --> 00:01:18.560
But my least dominant modality is the auditory. 
That's the one that I prefer the least.

00:01:18.740 --> 00:01:22.860
Now preferring a specific modality doesn't mean that 
that's the only way that you can learn.

00:01:22.860 --> 00:01:27.860
It doesn't mean that you can't learn 
in the other modalities. That just means that 
that's the preferred modality for you

00:01:27.860 --> 00:01:31.840
That's the one that engages you the most,
that's the one that you can relate to the most.

00:01:31.900 --> 00:01:33.900
So it's usually the best way for you to learn.

00:01:34.100 --> 00:01:39.840
Visual learners tend to prefer learning using
images, videos, charts, maps, graphs and so on.

00:01:40.020 --> 00:01:43.560
And often they see images in their mind
when they are reading or listening.

00:01:43.680 --> 00:01:49.420
Auditory learners prefer to learn by listening to other speak, 
by having discussions by explaining things out loud.

00:01:49.500 --> 00:01:53.620
Sometimes by recording a lesson and
listening to it again in order to review.

00:01:53.700 --> 00:01:59.340
Read/write learners learn visually but specifically 
through text and they enjoy learning through text books,

00:01:59.340 --> 00:02:06.260
through articles, through dictionary definitions, 
through writing journals, through writing articles, 
through writing blog posts in their target language.

00:02:06.500 --> 00:02:10.540
Read/write letters tend to be quite good with the patterns and the syntax of the language

00:02:10.540 --> 00:02:14.480
and they can look at a sentence and analyze 
the different elements within it quite easily.

00:02:14.560 --> 00:02:19.240
Kinesthetic learners are engaged when they are 
experiencing something physically with their body.

00:02:19.320 --> 00:02:24.400
They tend to like acting out real or simulated situations,
through movement and through role play.

00:02:24.540 --> 00:02:30.660
And since emotions are actually physical sensations,
kinesthetic learners are quite engaged by emotional sensations.

00:02:30.660 --> 00:02:33.700
So emotional impact will help 
in their learning and retention.

00:02:33.900 --> 00:02:37.180
You may already have a good idea of
what your preferred learning style is.

00:02:37.180 --> 00:02:43.440
But in case, you don't, you can take this quiz right here 
and get an idea of what your preferred learning style is.

00:02:43.440 --> 00:02:46.400
 

00:02:46.620 --> 00:02:51.020
When you know your preferred learning style, it becomes 
easier to understand why you're not motivated,

00:02:51.020 --> 00:02:55.460
why you're not excited to be learning and why
you're not learning as efficiently as you could be.

00:02:55.620 --> 00:02:59.160
Most people rely on their teacher 
or on materials to teach them.

00:02:59.260 --> 00:03:03.520
But your teacher and your materials often
don't target your preferred learning modalities.

00:03:03.640 --> 00:03:08.660
Have you ever had a teacher who just talked and talked 
and talked and talked and talked and talked .... ?

00:03:08.680 --> 00:03:14.160
Even if your teacher had a pleasant voice, unless you were 
an auditory learner, you were probably bored to death!

00:03:14.220 --> 00:03:20.800
Another common situation is that kinesthetic learners
are sometimes made to sit in their chair at their desk 
for 90 minutes straight.

00:03:20.800 --> 00:03:24.380
And, after about 30 minutes of that,
you're ready to pick up your chair and throw it.

00:03:27.360 --> 00:03:32.560
When I make videos for Langfocus, I try to include elements 
that address all of the different learning modalities,

00:03:32.560 --> 00:03:34.960
so that I can engage as many people as possible.

00:03:35.180 --> 00:03:40.940
For the visual modality, I try to include interesting images 
and backgrounds and charts and maps when appropriate.

00:03:40.980 --> 00:03:46.680
For auditory learners, 
I try to speak in a clear and engaging manner 
and include some music and sound effects.

00:03:46.760 --> 00:03:50.900
For read/write learners, I try to include some 
text headings and key points on the screen.

00:03:50.980 --> 00:03:55.960
And, for kinesthetic learners, I try to create some 
emotional effect through some of the things that I say

00:03:55.960 --> 00:04:02.020
and through some of the music and the way 
that I choose and mix it and threw
some symbols and imagery that I include in the video

00:04:02.160 --> 00:04:05.560
When you do self-study, you can choose to use 
your preferred learning modality

00:04:05.560 --> 00:04:09.820
to help you be more engaged with the material
and to help you retain the material better.

00:04:09.820 --> 00:04:18.380
If you're learning materials don't match your preferred
modality, then you can adapt the materials by filling in 
the gaps and creating the parts that are missing in your modality.

00:04:18.720 --> 00:04:22.800
For example, if you're a visual learner 
but your materials are mostly text based,

00:04:22.800 --> 00:04:27.540
then you can imagine images of the words 
and the sentences that you are practicing.

00:04:27.620 --> 00:04:33.900
Actually see the situation in your head 
as you are practicing the language, so that the
language and the imagery become associated.

00:04:34.200 --> 00:04:40.660
If you're an auditory learner, but your materials are 
mostly text-based, then you can imagine
the auditory component inside your head

00:04:40.720 --> 00:04:44.000
You can hear the voices, 
you can hear the voices being expressive.

00:04:44.320 --> 00:04:48.360
You can also chant sentences to yourself,
you can sing sentences to yourself.

00:04:48.540 --> 00:04:53.960
If you happen to be a read/write learner
and you are using an all audio program,
like Pimsleur for example,

00:04:54.100 --> 00:04:56.760
then you can try to imagine the words inside your head,

00:04:56.760 --> 00:05:00.260
actually see the letters of the words 
when you are saying them out loud.

00:05:00.400 --> 00:05:03.740
That might be hard to do at first, 
but when you get used to it, you can do it

00:05:03.820 --> 00:05:09.060
and then you will be connecting the auditory with 
your preferred learning modality of read/write.

00:05:09.220 --> 00:05:12.700
If you are a kinesthetic learner 
and your materials are mostly text-based,

00:05:12.880 --> 00:05:19.020
then you can imagine yourself going through 
that experience that you are reading about 
in that sentence or in that text.

00:05:19.160 --> 00:05:22.500
Try to actually feel what's going on 
and experience it in your body.

00:05:22.560 --> 00:05:25.840
Even though you're imagining it, 
you can try to feel those sensations.

00:05:26.000 --> 00:05:32.600
You can use body motions and gestures to learn 
new vocabulary and you can use body motions 
to act out the language that you are studying.

00:05:32.780 --> 00:05:36.460
You don't have to just stick to the learning materials 
that you have you can interpret them

00:05:36.460 --> 00:05:40.560
and adapt them to your own learning style 
and use them in the way that's best for you.

00:05:40.580 --> 00:05:46.280
So knowing your learning style is important 
because it can help you adapt materials 
and create new activities for yourself,

00:05:46.280 --> 00:05:49.740
to keep you engaged and help you 
retain the language that you are studying.

00:05:49.920 --> 00:05:54.640
So I recommend that you find out your learning style 
and you use it to your advantage today.

00:05:54.780 --> 00:05:58.360
Thank you for watching the Langfocus channel. 
Have a good day.

